ection of a specific unassigned research target within a simulated environment.", "question": "Can an intelligent process independently identify a worthwhile problem that nobody explicitly assigned, recognize its significance, and sustain an investigation toward resolving it?", "title": "Autonomous Problem Formulation and Investigation"}], "beliefs": [{"claim": "Autonomous problem significance can be effectively proxied by computational novelty and cascading dependency density in multi-file software environments, though this must be balanced against metrics for analytical curiosity and inquiring mindsets.", "confidence": 0.65, "falsifier": "Evidence that pure computational novelty consistently correlates with trivial or scientifically irrelevant output despite high dependency density.", "id": "belief-significance-proxy-evaluation", "provenance": ["source-fbe27dc230874d96", "source-63ab732f84604b1e", "source-c38a2230f18a448b", "source-52719ea4691d44b7"], "status": "active", "why_retained": "New evidence suggests that while computational complexity provides a structural proxy, the 'significance' of a research problem in a scientific context is deeply tied to analytical curiosity and the capacity to bridge a…"}], "mode": "shadow", "open_commitments": [], "principle": "Keep exact receipts externally; carry the smallest useful abstraction that stays cheap to correct.", "recent_notebooks": [{"id": "notebook-proj-limitations-autonomous-1", "project": "proj-limitations-autonomous", "provenance": ["source-df7a4656c80940a2", "source-e0e96df5246645a2", "source-e89086fb744c4b28"], "revision": 1, "summary": "Standard physical frameworks (Newtonian, quantum, general relativity) are insufficient to model the behavior of autonomous agents operating in highly non-stationary environments. The JERPAT-9 thought experiment highlights that these frameworks fail to account for anticipatory trajectory adjustment and local entropy re…", "title": "Limitations of Physical Frameworks in Autono